1
00:00:00,000 --> 00:00:03,560
If Propeller shows 72
confidence on a player prop,

2
00:00:03,840 --> 00:00:06,690
does that mean the pick
has a 72 percent chance of

3
00:00:06,700 --> 00:00:11,230
hitting? No. And the easiest way
to understand the difference is

4
00:00:11,240 --> 00:00:15,240
to see exactly where confidence
appears, what sits beside it,

5
00:00:15,340 --> 00:00:16,620
and what to inspect next.

6
00:00:17,660 --> 00:00:20,870
In this walkthrough, we’ll use the
same historical prop across the

7
00:00:20,880 --> 00:00:22,460
desktop and mobile platforms.

8
00:00:23,200 --> 00:00:25,490
The example on screen shows Luis Torrens,

9
00:00:25,500 --> 00:00:29,740
LESS than 0.5 RBIs, with 79 confidence.

10
00:00:30,380 --> 00:00:33,090
The number is different
from 72, but it is read in

11
00:00:33,100 --> 00:00:34,620
exactly the same way.

12
00:00:35,140 --> 00:00:37,330
Let’s start on the desktop Props Board.

13
00:00:37,340 --> 00:00:39,540
Each row keeps the player, market, line,

14
00:00:39,700 --> 00:00:41,960
direction, source, and
confidence together.

15
00:00:42,520 --> 00:00:45,840
Confidence helps you decide which
props may be worth opening first.

16
00:00:46,500 --> 00:00:49,840
It is a research-priority signal—not
a promise about the result.

17
00:00:50,220 --> 00:00:53,020
Select a player to open
the full Prop Detail page.

18
00:00:53,480 --> 00:00:56,320
At the top, Propeller shows
the player and market.

19
00:00:56,960 --> 00:01:01,520
In this example, the
recommendation is LESS 0.5 RBIs.

20
00:01:01,780 --> 00:01:02,840
The direction is LESS.

21
00:01:03,260 --> 00:01:05,290
The line is 0.5.

22
00:01:05,300 --> 00:01:07,500
Those two pieces must
always be read together.

23
00:01:08,320 --> 00:01:11,350
On desktop, you can find the
line beside the recommendation at

24
00:01:11,360 --> 00:01:12,360
the top of the page.

25
00:01:12,880 --> 00:01:15,630
It also appears in the
recommendation card and again in the

26
00:01:15,640 --> 00:01:16,880
market panel on the right.

27
00:01:17,360 --> 00:01:21,310
Repeating the line matters
because a move from 0.5 to 1.5

28
00:01:21,320 --> 00:01:22,900
creates a different question.

29
00:01:23,420 --> 00:01:26,430
On mobile, the same information
is placed near the top of

30
00:01:26,440 --> 00:01:30,720
Pick Detail. LESS 0.5
appears beside the stat,

31
00:01:30,940 --> 00:01:34,550
and the Line Check card repeats
this line while comparing it

32
00:01:34,560 --> 00:01:36,180
with the available market average.

33
00:01:36,720 --> 00:01:39,810
Before using any score, confirm
that the line on your platform

34
00:01:39,820 --> 00:01:42,220
still matches the line Propeller analyzed.

35
00:01:43,100 --> 00:01:44,040
Now look at confidence.

36
00:01:44,560 --> 00:01:48,220
On desktop, confidence appears
in its own summary card.

37
00:01:48,480 --> 00:01:50,640
This historical example shows 79.

38
00:01:51,220 --> 00:01:54,550
On mobile, the same score is
displayed in the confidence ring

39
00:01:54,560 --> 00:01:57,620
at the upper right and
again inside Model Read.

40
00:01:58,220 --> 00:02:00,820
A score of 72 would be
interpreted the same way:

41
00:02:01,200 --> 00:02:04,770
the model sees a comparatively strong
directional signal for the side

42
00:02:04,780 --> 00:02:08,300
displayed. It does not mean
a 72 percent chance to hit,

43
00:02:08,480 --> 00:02:10,180
and it does not guarantee the outcome.

44
00:02:10,860 --> 00:02:13,520
The mobile Model Read makes that
distinction especially clear.

45
00:02:14,100 --> 00:02:17,120
Confidence and chance to hit are
displayed as separate measurements.

46
00:02:17,780 --> 00:02:21,980
Confidence summarizes the strength and
agreement of the model’s directional evidence.

47
00:02:22,620 --> 00:02:24,320
Chance to hit is a separate estimate.

48
00:02:25,020 --> 00:02:26,780
Do not substitute one for the other.

49
00:02:27,300 --> 00:02:29,620
Next, review the evidence
behind the recommendation.

50
00:02:30,660 --> 00:02:33,060
On desktop, scroll to Why This Lean.

51
00:02:33,680 --> 00:02:36,760
This section turns the model
output into readable reasons,

52
00:02:37,080 --> 00:02:41,160
such as recent usage, matchup
context, performance against the line,

53
00:02:41,440 --> 00:02:45,220
or role-related information.
Below that is the Model Breakdown.

54
00:02:45,760 --> 00:02:49,390
These are the independent analysis
agents that contribute to the final

55
00:02:49,400 --> 00:02:52,240
direction. Depending on
the sport and market,

56
00:02:52,480 --> 00:02:54,680
you may see agents covering
market information,

57
00:02:55,080 --> 00:02:58,920
recent hit rate, injuries,
matchup, venue, usage,

58
00:02:59,240 --> 00:03:01,740
lineup status, and other relevant factors.

59
00:03:02,460 --> 00:03:04,580
The value is not that
every agent always agrees.

60
00:03:05,040 --> 00:03:07,390
The value is that you can see
where the recommendation came

61
00:03:07,400 --> 00:03:11,630
from. Broad agreement is different
from one strong signal surrounded by

62
00:03:11,640 --> 00:03:15,970
conflicting evidence. The mobile
platform presents the same idea in Model

63
00:03:15,980 --> 00:03:19,280
Read. It identifies the
pick side, the top driver,

64
00:03:19,740 --> 00:03:22,000
the level of agreement,
and the signal mix.

65
00:03:22,560 --> 00:03:26,350
Continue down the page to Agent
Breakdown to inspect the contributing

66
00:03:26,360 --> 00:03:30,360
agents individually. This is
where confidence becomes useful.

67
00:03:31,080 --> 00:03:34,390
A stronger score means the available
signals support the displayed direction

68
00:03:34,400 --> 00:03:37,240
more strongly. It does
not remove uncertainty.

69
00:03:37,800 --> 00:03:40,300
Lineups change. Roles change.

70
00:03:40,700 --> 00:03:44,240
Markets move. Players leave games early.

71
00:03:44,840 --> 00:03:48,270
Sports outcomes also
contain real variance.

72
00:03:48,280 --> 00:03:50,240
That is why Propeller
should begin your research,

73
00:03:50,420 --> 00:03:53,940
not end it. Here is a practical
workflow for new users.

74
00:03:54,760 --> 00:03:57,090
First, scan the Props Board
and open a prop you want

75
00:03:57,100 --> 00:04:01,020
to understand. Second, confirm
the direction and exact line.

76
00:04:01,440 --> 00:04:05,260
On this example, that means LESS 0.5 RBIs.

77
00:04:05,620 --> 00:04:09,090
Third, find the confidence score,
then keep it separate from any

78
00:04:09,100 --> 00:04:13,890
displayed chance-to-hit estimate.
Fourth, inspect Why This Lean,

79
00:04:13,900 --> 00:04:16,180
Model Read, and the agent breakdown.

80
00:04:16,920 --> 00:04:19,450
Look for the factors doing the
work and whether the evidence

81
00:04:19,460 --> 00:04:24,530
broadly agrees. Fifth, verify the
current line and player context on

82
00:04:24,540 --> 00:04:26,740
the platform where you
make your own decision.

83
00:04:27,520 --> 00:04:30,040
If the line has moved or
the context has changed,

84
00:04:30,380 --> 00:04:33,170
the original analysis may no
longer answer the same question.

85
00:04:33,180 --> 00:04:38,510
And finally, decide whether the prop
deserves more research—or whether

86
00:04:38,520 --> 00:04:41,900
to skip it. Skipping is
always a valid outcome.

87
00:04:42,560 --> 00:04:45,760
So, what does 72 confidence actually mean?

88
00:04:46,500 --> 00:04:50,550
It means Propeller sees a comparatively
strong directional signal for the

89
00:04:50,560 --> 00:04:54,480
displayed side. It is not a
72 percent win probability.

90
00:04:55,160 --> 00:04:56,020
It is not a guarantee.

91
00:04:56,600 --> 00:04:59,010
And it is most useful
when you read it with the

92
00:04:59,020 --> 00:05:02,180
direction, the line, and the
evidence behind the score.

93
00:05:03,120 --> 00:05:06,910
Use the desktop platform for the
full research workspace and the

94
00:05:06,920 --> 00:05:09,920
mobile companion for quick
access to the same core analysis.

95
00:05:10,620 --> 00:05:14,300
To explore today’s player props,
visit Propeller Picks dot com.

96
